WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.700
How can a Turing machine

2
00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.640
test whether a unary length

3
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:04.860
is a power of two?

4
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.280
Repeated halving accepts when one

5
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.500
active zero remains and rejects

6
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:10.820
an odd count greater than

7
00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.060
one. We will separate that

8
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.040
concept from the lecture diagram’s

9
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.540
concrete convention, which blanks its

10
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.420
first zero as a left-end

11
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:19.800
reference.

12
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.580
In the high-level algorithm, sweep

13
00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:24.640
across active zeros crossing out

14
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.580
every other one. If the

15
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.340
count was even, the remaining

16
00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:29.760
count is half as large;

17
00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:31.260
if it was odd and

18
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.900
greater than one, reject. Returning

19
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.080
to the left end and

20
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.540
repeating eventually reaches one exactly

21
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.480
for a power-of-two length.

22
00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:41.700
For the exact diagram on

23
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.660
four zeros, q one blanks

24
00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:44.800
the first cell and enters

25
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:46.000
q two at the second

26
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.220
zero. This special first cell

27
00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.960
is the left-end reference, so

28
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:51.680
the remaining explicit zeros are

29
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.300
not the entire conceptual count.

30
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.240
Do not mix that bookkeeping

31
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.300
with an unrelated simplified animation.

32
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.020
From q two, cross out

33
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:01.540
the next zero and move

34
00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.640
right into q three. In

35
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.160
q three, retain the following

36
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.420
zero and move into q

37
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.920
four. In q four, cross

38
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.200
out the next zero and

39
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.480
return to q three at

40
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.620
the right-hand blank, leaving blank,

41
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.280
x, zero, x on the

42
00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:16.540
tape.

43
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.720
The right-hand blank in q

44
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.020
three signals a successful sweep

45
00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:22.520
and turns the machine left

46
00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.340
in q five. It passes

47
00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.900
back over x and zero

48
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.040
until the left reference, then

49
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.560
moves right into q two.

50
00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.800
Those return moves do not

51
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.580
change which zeros were crossed

52
00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:33.960
out.

53
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.120
On the next sweep, q

54
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:37.700
two skips the x and

55
00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.640
crosses out the remaining explicit

56
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.660
zero. The tape becomes blank

57
00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:43.080
followed by three x markers,

58
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:44.540
and the machine returns left

59
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.780
again. Finally q two skips

60
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.160
all three x markers and

61
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:49.800
reaches blank with no unmatched

62
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.780
explicit zero, so the diagram

63
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:52.220
accepts.

64
00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.240
Check the shortest boundary inputs

65
00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.260
rather than extrapolating from four

66
00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.440
zeros. A single zero is

67
00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:01.040
accepted because its length is

68
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.740
two to the zero, while

69
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.420
epsilon is rejected. Two zeros

70
00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:08.320
also reduce successfully, whereas three

71
00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.580
reach the diagram’s odd-count rejection

72
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:11.000
case.

73
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.560
The halting argument relies on

74
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.360
progress. A successful nonterminal round

75
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.660
reduces the conceptual active count,

76
00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.560
and every finite sweep and

77
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.000
return finishes. Eventually the machine

78
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.960
reaches an accepting singleton or

79
00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:26.400
an odd count that rejects,

80
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.800
so it is a decider

81
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.340
rather than a mere recognizer.

82
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:32.100
Halving does not imply logarithmic

83
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.320
tape-running time. There are only

84
00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.420
logarithmically many successful rounds, but

85
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.420
each involves scans across cells

86
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.840
and markers on the original

87
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.260
tape span. A straightforward bound

88
00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.880
counts those scans as well;

89
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:46.900
naming the number of rounds

90
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.380
alone ignores most primitive moves.

91
00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.200
Things to take note of.

92
00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.380
Use the actual diagram’s initial

93
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.000
blank and state conventions when

94
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.020
writing configurations, and keep the

95
00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:59.400
conceptual halving explanation distinct from

96
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.680
that exact trace. Test epsilon,

97
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.280
one, two and odd lengths,

98
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.580
explain termination, and never turn

99
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:07.280
a scan-based machine into a

100
00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.340
constant-cost arithmetic operation.
